## H2 branch discovery, inherited prefix and isolation

Reviewed `source/discovery.rs`, `protocol/target_source.rs`, `protocol/target_mapping.rs`, their readers/cursors, spool and lineage call chain, and changed branch tests.

Discovery admits auxiliary parents only through actual history_base references. Filename/header agreement remains checked; target head selection excludes foreign parent conversations. Parent closure, ambiguous candidates, missing links and cycles remain bounded/fail-closed. Original C's parent being filtered out is consistent with the baseline mechanism documented by S1/S2.

`target_source.rs:978` binds the verified parent and plans `[0, end_byte_offset)` for a cross-conversation edge. `:1569` uses complete-line bounded windows; per-window segment and aggregate decoded limits remain separate. Initial reads and append lineage recovery both process these windows; previous record windows go through the existing spool, preserving chronological replay. `:1654` requires complete range, no parse gap/pending tail/shrink and an exact final ordinal/byte cut. Parent changes after that cut do not enter the independent child. Source provenance retains the physical conversation while event target binding uses the requested child.

`target_mapping.rs:872` separates cross-conversation inheritance from same-conversation last-turn replacement. Independent Branch adds validated inheritance evidence without a replacement mutation. Existing same-conversation validation is retained. No Node locator input, new analyzer mode, second collection chain or external rollout write was introduced.

The original S3 R1 is real: `s3/review-r1/repro-result.json` records a 5542-byte inherited prefix rejected by a 4096-byte segment limit even though the parent itself could be processed in windows. Current code and the focused test prove the later shared-window repair. Original failure evidence remains historical; it was not rewritten into PASS. Total-budget and overlong-line failures remain expected protection, not repaired-away success criteria.

## H3 and 833 prerequisite compatibility

Reviewed both changed vendor carriers and their validation, facade/profile, Adapter, owned AnalysisEvent, reducer and target process/checkpoint profile propagation. Five directly changed vendor source files checked in `rust-audit.json` are byte-identical between Spike and formal; upstream revision, local patch declaration and repository-relative closure are retained.

Ordinary nonempty call_id outputs retain ToolCompleted behavior. Delegation requires the exact missing-call-id native shape, supported name/namespace, real id and full string body. Null, blank, wrong type and unsupported shape remain invalid. `codex_adapter/mod.rs:1380` verifies explicit versus active turn and completed carrier versus verified physical SessionMeta binding; explicit target identity remains separate from inherited physical identity.

`analysis.rs:1125` compares immutable physical facts before duplicate suppression, validates same logical id/body/turn/tool/binding, preserves both carriers and selects response_item as logical representative. Conflicts preserve partial coverage; the mapper rechecks projection qualification before canonical output. No fake tool identity/detail, ordinary user input or successful diagnostic substitute was introduced.

The actual worker passes negotiated profile through decoder/Adapter/mapper/lineage/checkpoint. Old capability intersection encountering required delegation fails before batch. Nonempty old cursors are preserved locally; new-profile ordinary reread starts a new job with empty recovery fields. Cross-profile checkpoint restore is refused. Same-v2 retry uses identical payload and delivery identity.

An important boundary is **not independently proved here**: an old unsettled initial delivery with no persisted anchor has no profile identifier inside recovery. `target_runtime.rs:131` therefore selects from hello capabilities. The 833/S3 execution/review explicitly makes Node responsible for preserving the original hello/profile for this case; Rust cannot infer it from segment/batch ids. This was forwarded to the Node reviewer for consumer audit. It is not evidence of comprehensive old-state compatibility, nor a Rust-only finding without checking the consumer. The implemented v1-anchor path and same-v2 retry are covered.

Cross-check from the Node reviewer: a normal terminal reconciliation carrying an old canonical target-v1 anchor must not be converted into a new-profile append. Rust's runtime guard at `target_runtime.rs:522` and mapper guard at `target_mapping.rs:1098` correctly reject it. Shared supplement §5.2 assigns the fresh new-job/four-empty-fields backfill choice to the consumer. A consumer path that always appends from that old checkpoint would be a separate compatibility defect; changing the Rust guard would violate the confirmed boundary. Node's concrete trigger/reproduction and finding ownership remain with that reviewer.

